WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:04.560
I help some thankful
developers come to terms

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.010
with resident instances.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.520
Is their performance cup
half full or half empty?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.430
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.930
I was attending a
meetup last week

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.640
where I was approached
by some developers

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.770
at a company called
Think Thanks,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.890
a nonprofit whose goal
is to improve the quality

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.720
and frequency of thanks given
to people and organizations.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.920
They were a great
group of folks who

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.860
built their service
on App Engine

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.720
and had already done all of
the cold-boot optimization

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.369
that we could
possibly talk about.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.970
Still, they were seeing issues
when sudden traffic came in.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.390
Their problem was that the app
traffic was extremely bursty.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.700
They would go from
zero users for hours

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.200
to suddenly thousands.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.180
And in those
situations, instances

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.310
were legitimately
being cold booted,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.350
causing a delay in
overall performance

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.300
while that instance
was being created.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.640
The latency of Think Thanks
was taking considerable hit

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.910
any time a new instance
was being booted up.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.040
So besides just
adjusting the scheduler

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.020
to boot up less
instances, it looks

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.420
like they needed something
stronger to handle

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.395
sudden spikes in traffic.

00:01:05.395 --> 00:01:07.770
There was really only one
thing left to suggest to them--

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.360
resident instances.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.360
As I talked about before, the
App Engine serving algorithms

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.370
are constantly deciding
on whether it's better

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.487
to queue a request or
spin up a new instance.

00:01:17.487 --> 00:01:19.320
This takes into account
a significant number

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.870
of factors to decide
when this should happen.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.780
If your cold boot time
is significant, then

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.730
during extreme
spikes in traffic,

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.190
request latency will
suffer as those requests

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.310
sit longer in the work
queue before being serviced.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.780
This could flood
your application

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.820
with a huge amount
of startup costs

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.800
and delay the entire
system significantly.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.980
This is where resident
instances come in.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.190
A resident instance is
one that stays alive 24/7

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.720
and only receives traffic
under a very specific situation

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.030
while a new instance
is booting up.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.120
Basically, resident instances
are spare instances.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.600
While a new instance
is booting up,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.230
they handle the excess
load that the new instance

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.539
would be handling.

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:01.080
Once it's up and
running, the traffic

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.080
is sent to the new instance
and the resident one

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.560
can go back to waiting around.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.210
Which is really
important because it

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.190
means that user
requests won't be

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.680
subject to sudden
spikes in latency that

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.520
come from cold-boot performance,
which is exactly what I saw

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.850
when we changed the main idle
instances flag of Think Thanks

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:18.690
to one.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.340
Once the new request
load came in,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.230
it was handled by
the existing instance

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.360
so that latency didn't
shoot through the roof

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.830
while the new one
was booting up.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.670
The takeaway?

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.610
Resident instances are
the key to rapid scale

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.830
and not having your user
perception of latency

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:32.910
be impacted.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.587
But after playing around with
resident instances a bit,

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:37.170
Think Thanks noticed
that there seemed

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.590
to be a plus-one tax involved
in situations where traffic

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.730
was lower than normal.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.190
Basically, if no one had
been using their application

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.780
for a while, a
sudden burst would

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.510
cost two instances,
the idle instance

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.670
as well as the new
instance being spun up.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.270
But for infrequent traffic, that
really wasn't what they needed.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.700
Think Thanks was
wasting an instance.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.940
An alternative solution
came from a Google post

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.610
where a community member
described a similar problem.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.770
His startup process might
take multiple seconds

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.030
in order to fill a database.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.310
But rather than using
resident instances,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.760
he created and used a cron
job to ping the instance

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.710
and keep it alive indefinitely.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.240
When we tried this
on Think Thanks,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.340
we saw pretty much
what we expected.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.860
In the lowest volume
setting, this setup

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.740
allowed their back end to keep
from spawning a new instance

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.410
when it didn't need to.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.660
Happy with their latency
improvement, the Think Thanks

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.040
folks finished their
meetup-provided pizza

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.230
and then went happily
along their way.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.480
Afterwards, I talked to
a few App Engine experts

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.260
who wanted to make it very clear
that there's two very distinct

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.390
and separate use cases here.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.330
If you have a high
frequency, lots of instance

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.000
churn scenario and a
slow cold boot time,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.520
then resident instances
are your best bet.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.280
However, if you have a
low frequency traffic

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.050
with occasional bursts
in a slow cold boot time,

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:54.900
then keeping an
always-cron instance around

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.210
is ideal since you can
handle the initial traffic

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.150
load gracefully with a lower
number of total instances.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.950
The downside to both
of these situations

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.096
is that these persistent
instances come at a cost.

00:04:05.096 --> 00:04:06.720
You're basically
paying for an instance

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.170
to be alive 24/7, which
could add significant costs

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.800
to your operating budget.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.880
Which is why I like to say that
you should always carefully

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.950
balance your tradeoffs
before making decisions

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.200
to go forward with any
performance improvements.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.475
And to understand what
types of trade there are,

00:04:21.475 --> 00:04:23.850
make sure to check out the
rest of the Google Cloud Atlas

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:24.480
videos.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.490
Because when it
comes to your users,

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:27.540
every millisecond counts.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

